s the wealthiest person of all time, although there are no reliable ways to accurately calculate his wealth.", "newValue": "Sunjata Keïta (c. 1215–1255) stands as the legendary founder of the Mali Empire, a monumental West African polity that dominated the region for centuries. Emerging from the Mandinka heartland in the early 13th century, Keïta unified disparate chiefdoms and established a robust administrative and military structure that laid the groundwork for one of history's wealthiest and most culturally influential empires. His reign marked a pivotal transition from fragmented local polities to a cohesive, expansive state, fundamentally reshaping the political and economic landscape of Medieval West Africa.

The ascent of Sunjata Keïta culminated in the decisive Battle of Kirina around 1235 CE, where his forces triumphed over Soumaoro Kanté, the powerful sorcerer-king of the Sosso. Following this victory, Keïta consolidated his authority, establishing Niani as the capital and presiding over the promulgation of the Manden Charter (Kouroukan Fouga), an oral constitution outlining rights, responsibilities, and social organization for the newly formed empire. His military campaigns extended Malian control over strategic gold-producing regions and vital trade routes, integrating diverse ethnic groups under a common political framework.

Keïta's organizational genius and military prowess created an enduring state that profoundly influenced subsequent West African history. The Mali Empire, built on his foundations, facilitated the flourishing of trans-Saharan trade, the spread of Islam, and the development of major intellectual and commercial centers like Timbuktu and Djenné. His legacy directly enabled the golden age of the empire under his great-nephew, Mansa Musa, whose legendary pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 showcased Mali's immense wealth and power to the wider world."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-17T21:12:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44176681984", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Educated at in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Influenced by in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}, {"title": "Born into in the 500 – 1500 CE", "type": "Person", "year": "500"}], "newValue": ["The decline of the Ghana Empire created a power vacuum and instability across the Western Sudan.", "The oppressive expansionist policies of Soumaoro Kanté, king of the Sosso, spurred Mandinka resistance and a desire for unified leadership.", "The fragmentation of Mandinka chiefdoms necessitated a strong leader to forge a cohesive political and military alliance."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-17T21:12:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44176681984", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Founded through Sunjata Keïta's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Wrote through Sunjata Keïta's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Established through Sunjata Keïta's historical significance", "type": "Person"}, {"title": "Transformed through Sunjata Keïta's historical significance", "type": "Person"}], "newValue": ["Sunjata Keïta established the Mali Empire, which became the dominant political and economic power in West Africa for centuries.", "His reign led to the promulgation of the Manden Charter